Q: SOAP aur REST mein kya difference hai?

SOAP (Simple Object Access Protocol) and REST (Representational State Transfer) are two ways to build web services / APIs.
SOAP is a protocol; REST is an architectural style.

────────────────────────────────────────
Quick comparison

| Point              | SOAP                                      | REST                                      |
| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| Type               | Protocol (strict rules)                   | Architectural style                       |
| Message format     | XML only (SOAP envelope)                  | JSON (common), XML, text, etc.            |
| Transport          | HTTP, SMTP, TCP… (often HTTP)             | Usually HTTP/HTTPS                        |
| Contract           | WSDL (strong contract)                    | OpenAPI/Swagger (optional, looser)        |

────────────────────────────────────────
What is WSDL?

WSDL = Web Services Description Language

It is an XML document that describes a SOAP web service completely — like a formal API contract.

WSDL tells the client:
- What operations/methods exist (e.g. transferMoney, getBalance)
- What input/output messages look like (XML structure / types)
- Where the service is (endpoint URL)
- How to call it (protocol / SOAP binding)

Think of it as:
SOAP service ka “menu + address + rules” in one XML file.

Rough structure (concept):
- types      → data structures (XSD)
- message    → request/response parts
- portType   → operations (like an interface)
- binding    → how operations map to SOAP/HTTP
- service    → actual endpoint URL

Example idea:
Client reads BankService.wsdl
  → generates stub/proxy code
  → calls transferMoney() as if it were a local method
  → under the hood SOAP XML goes over the network

REST comparison:
- SOAP → WSDL (required for classic SOAP tooling)
- REST → OpenAPI / Swagger (optional but common)

Why interviews care:
WSDL = strong, machine-readable contract → good for banks/enterprise where client and server must agree strictly on message format.
| Standards          | WS-Security, WS-ReliableMessaging, etc.   | HTTPS + app-level auth (JWT, OAuth)       |
| Style              | Operation / RPC oriented (verbs)          | Resource oriented (nouns + HTTP methods)  |
| Payload size       | Heavier (XML verbose)                     | Lighter (especially JSON)                 |
| Caching            | Harder                                    | Natural with HTTP GET caching             |
| Browser / mobile   | Less friendly                             | Very friendly                             |
| Typical use        | Banks, legacy enterprise, strict contracts| Most modern public APIs / microservices   |

────────────────────────────────────────
SOAP example idea

POST /TransferService
Content-Type: text/xml

<soap:Envelope>
  <soap:Body>
    <TransferMoney>
      <from>101</from>
      <to>202</to>
      <amount>500</amount>
    </TransferMoney>
  </soap:Body>
</soap:Envelope>

────────────────────────────────────────
REST example idea

POST /accounts/101/transfers
Content-Type: application/json

{ "toAccountId": 202, "amount": 500 }

GET    /users/1
POST   /users
PUT    /users/1
DELETE /users/1

────────────────────────────────────────
When choose what?

Choose SOAP when:
- Enterprise / bank needs formal WSDL contract
- Advanced WS-* security / reliable messaging required
- Existing SOAP legacy systems

Choose REST when:
- Modern microservices, mobile, public APIs
- Need speed, simplicity, JSON, HTTP caching
- Easier tooling with Spring Boot (@RestController)

────────────────────────────────────────
✅ Short Interview Answer

SOAP ek protocol hai jo mostly XML + WSDL use karta hai — strict contract, heavier, enterprise/legacy mein common.
REST ek architectural style hai — resources + HTTP methods, usually JSON, lightweight, modern APIs ke liye preferred.
SOAP = protocol + XML; REST = style + HTTP resources.
